Title:
Defender Reversing – ProcOpen (Qwen)

Type:
Blog Post

Short Summary (4-8 sentences max):
This post reverse engineers why Microsoft Defender’s `MsMpEng.exe` opens handles to other processes via `OpenProcess()` during real-time protection. Using ETW telemetry (Kernel-Audit-API-Calls) and a captured call stack, it walks through `mpengine.dll` functions that lead from engine command dispatch to behavioral monitoring (BM) “sync process start” scanning and ultimately deep process inspection. The author uses Ghidra + GhidraMCP with the Qwen model to annotate likely function roles (e.g., process scan engine, HIPS/ASR evaluation, Lua rule checks, reputation, and memory inspection). A key focus is the “memory inspection” path: Defender opens the target with `PROCESS_QUERY_INFORMATION | PROCESS_VM_READ` (0x600), queries an undocumented `NtQueryInformationProcess` info class (0x60), then dispatches inspection commands (0x60/0x57) to a BM controller (likely involving kernel components). It also highlights uncertainty where reverse engineering cannot prove semantics (e.g., exact meaning of the 0x60 bitmask), and suggests avenues to validate (kernel tracing, handler analysis, PDBs). Useful for red teamers and malware analysts trying to understand Defender inspection triggers, and for defenders validating telemetry and detection logic around process-handle opens.

Technical Focus:
- ETW tracing of `OpenProcess` (Microsoft-Windows-Kernel-Audit-API-Calls / `PspLogAuditOpenProcessEvent`)
- Reverse engineering `mpengine.dll` call chains (BM pipeline / engine dispatch)
- Behavioral Monitoring (BM), HIPS/ASR rule gating, Lua-based detections, reputation checks
- Deep process inspection via `OpenProcess` + `PROCESS_VM_READ` and controller dispatch
- Undocumented `NtQueryInformationProcess` info class `0x60` and feature gating by OS version
- Caching/lookup mechanisms (hash tables, FNV-1a, SRW locks) for scan results

Use Cases:
- Map Defender’s process-inspection pipeline to specific ETW events and call stacks
- Identify which Defender components/features trigger `OpenProcess(PROCESS_VM_READ)`
- Build more accurate detections/telemetry correlations for Defender-driven process access
- Guide reverse engineering efforts (where to look next: BM controller handlers, kernel paths, symbols)
- Evaluate potential evasion ideas by understanding when deep inspection is invoked (conceptual, not a bypass recipe)

Keywords:
Microsoft Defender, MsMpEng.exe, mpengine.dll, OpenProcess, NtOpenProcess, PROCESS_VM_READ, PROCESS_QUERY_INFORMATION, ETW, Microsoft-Windows-Kernel-Audit-API-Calls, PspLogAuditOpenProcessEvent, RedEdr, Ghidra, GhidraMCP, Qwen, behavioral monitoring, HIPS, ASR, Lua signatures, reputation lookup, NtQueryInformationProcess, ProcessInformationClass 0x60, SRW lock, FNV-1a hash, WdFilter.sys